  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 145 PM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nCurrently a cold front is sweeping through the state west to\neast, clearing out the low stratus clouds. Behind the front\nwinds are gusting up to 30mph. This trend will continue through\nthe evening. In the low stratus mist and sprinkles are falling,\nreducing visibilities at times. Aloft a large broad trough has\nformed across the Pacific Northwest. The rest of this weekend\nwill be in the 60s to lower 70s. That broad trough will bring\nsouthwest flow back into the state Sunday, leading to showers\nand thunderstorms beginning Sunday evening south of the\ninterstate. After sunset, the chances will increase as a\ndisturbance from the Rockies forms a surface low and moves into\nthe Dakotas. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms will continue through\nMonday as a shortwave and front move in behind the low. There is\na chance for thunderstorms as there will be 200-500 J/kg of\nMUCAPE available Sunday night with 40-50kts of shear. Severe\nstorms cannot be ruled out Sunday with a potential low level jet\nacross the Dakotas, but as of now it remains a very low risk.\nMonday some models have a small axis of 1000 J/kg of MUCAPE but\nothers don't, so again low confidence in severe storms Monday,\nbut not zero. Monday with the surface low in northern South\nDakota cold air funnels or even a legit tornado could maybe be \npossible but it would be a very conditional risk. Through these 3 \nperiod there are medium to high chances of at least 1 inch of rain. \n\nThe rest of the week zonal flow takes over the northern CONUS,\nkeeping temperatures seasonal in the mid 60s. Afternoon\nhumidity values will lower back under 50 percent. Overnight lows\nwill hang around the mid 40s and possibly upper 30s in the far\nnorth. Looking towards next weekend another shortwave could move\nin, lowering highs into the 50s with a chance of rain.",
